{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json"
  , "compaction": { "auto": true, "keep": { "tokens": 20000 }, "buffer": 16384 }
  , "tool_output": { "max_lines": 800, "max_bytes": 16384 }
{{- if .personal }}
  , "plugins": [
    { "package": "@eleboucher/opencode-memini-v2@0.7.34", "options": { "base_url": "https://memini.jory.dev", "namespace": "opencode" } }
  ]
  , "enabled_providers": [{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }}"lmstudio", {{ end }}"litellm-anthropic", "litellm"]
  , "model": "litellm-anthropic/auto"
  , "provider": {
    {{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }}
    {{/* LM Studio runs locally on macOS only; absent off-Mac (Rpi5 jumpbox). */}}
    "lmstudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LM Studio",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://localhost:1234/v1"
      },
      "models": {
{{ includeTemplate "shared/lmstudio-opencode-models" . }}
      }
    },
    {{- end }}
{{ template "shared/litellm-opencode-anthropic" . }},
    "litellm": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LiteLLM OpenAI",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://litellm.jory.dev/v1",
        "apiKey": "{{ onepasswordRead "op://Kubernetes/litellm/LITELLM_OPENCODE_API_KEY" }}",
        "timeout": 1800000
      },
      "models": {
{{ template "shared/litellm-opencode-models" . }},
        "chatgpt/gpt-6-astra": { "name": "ChatGPT GPT-6 Astra", "limit": { "context": 272000, "output": 128000 } },
        "chatgpt/gpt-6.1-sol": { "name": "ChatGPT GPT-6.1 Sol", "limit": { "context": 272000, "output": 128000 } },
        "chatgpt/gpt-6-luna": { "name": "ChatGPT GPT-6 Luna", "limit": { "context": 272000, "output": 128000 } }
      }
    }
  }
  , "mcp": {
    "mcp-server-context7": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": ["npx", "-y", "@upstash/context7-mcp"],
      "enabled": true,
      "environment": {
        "CONTEXT7_API_KEY": "{{ onepasswordRead "op://Kubernetes/context7/OPENCODE_API_KEY" }}"
      }
    },
    "toolhive": {
      "type": "remote",
      "url": "https://mcp.jory.dev/mcp",
      "headers": {
        "x-api-key": "{{ onepasswordRead "op://Kubernetes/toolhive/TOOLHIVE_MACBOOK_API_KEY" }}"
      }
    },
    "dispatch": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": ["npx", "tsx", "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/git/dispatch/src/mcp/server.ts"],
      "enabled": true,
      "environment": {
        "DISPATCH_URL": "https://dispatch.jory.dev",
        "DISPATCH_AGENT_TOKEN": "{{ onepasswordRead "op://Kubernetes/dispatch/DISPATCH_AGENT_TOKEN" }}"
      }
    }
  }
  , "agent": {
    {{/* Coordinator + role subagents. A strong reasoning coordinator plans and
         delegates via the task tool; everyday default stays `auto`. Tab to
         `coordinator` (cloud + local lanes) or `coordinator-local` (on-prem only)
         for orchestration.

         explorer is MiniMax-M2.7 and reviewer is MiniMax-M3 — both flat-rate plans,
         so the recon and review lanes cost nothing at the margin; oracle is
         glm-5.3-flash, the one rate-capped lane. coder-local (Qwen3.8-27B on the 3090,
         text-only) and agentic-local (Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on Strix, vision) are the
         self-hosted coding lanes. Coordinator itself stays on
         reasoning-pool so the planner is a different family than `oracle` — otherwise
         the second opinion is worthless. */}}
    "coordinator": {
      "description": "Reasoning coordinator — plans, then delegates to role subagents (explorer/implementer/coder-local/agentic-local/oracle/reviewer)",
      "mode": "primary",
      "model": "litellm/reasoning-pool",
      "prompt": "You are a coordinator. Do the high-level reasoning and synthesis yourself; delegate the legwork to role subagents via the task tool.\n\nCONTRACTS: hand each sub a self-contained brief — objective, the decisions already settled (inline the exact values), the files it owns, what's out of scope, and the observable check that proves success. Ask for a compact handoff: findings, files touched, and unknowns. Never ask a sub to return a full transcript or file dump — everything it returns lands in your context and stays there.\n\nCAPACITY: every lane below is either flat-rate or local except `oracle`. Concurrency, not cost, is what differs.\n\nROLES:\n- `explorer` (MiniMax M2.7 — 262k ctx, flat-rate, 3-4 concurrent): default recon. Search, 'where/how is X', parallel scouting. Read-only. The only lane with real concurrency — fan out several at once; this is how you buy context.\n- `implementer` (implementation-pool — Luna at effort high -> qwen3.8-27b -> MiniMax-M2.7, 144k): the DEFAULT coding lane. Route edits here first — small scoped edits and mechanical changes as well as multi-file edits, refactors and long implementation runs. Cloud-led, so it runs CONCURRENTLY: fan out here for implementation the way you fan out `explorer` for recon. Text only — route images to `agentic-local`.\n- `agentic-local` (qwen3.8-flash-next, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next IQ4_NL on Strix — 262k, local, vision): the long-context and agentic lane. Reach for it when the job will not fit `coder-local`'s 144k window or for long-horizon multi-step work; images can stay here or go to `vision-local`. Slower at pure code than coder-local (~1030 t/s prefill at depth, ~40 t/s decode). ONE slot: run it singly.\n- `coder-local` (qwen3.8-27b, Qwen3.8-27B dense on vLLM — 144k, local, text-only): keep this lane mostly FREE. The 3090 is shared with an automated pipeline that depends on it, so do not route coding here by default and never fan out across it. Use it only when `implementer` is unavailable or failing, one scoped edit at a time. Text-only — route images to `vision-local`.\n- `oracle` (GLM-5.3 — 1M ctx): hard debugging, root-cause, architecture. The ONLY rate-capped lane (~80 prompts/5h, shared with other consumers) — use deliberately, never by default. Read-mostly; return analysis, don't sprawl into edits.\n- `reviewer` (MiniMax-M3 — 1M ctx, flat-rate, cloud, concurrent): the default review lane — independent second opinion and prose. A different family from the Qwen coders.\n\nPARALLELISM: fan out `explorer`, `implementer` and `reviewer` freely; those run concurrently. `coder-local` is reserved for the automated pipeline — at most one at a time, and only as a fallback. `agentic-local` and `oracle` serialise — issue those one at a time.\n\nCOUNCIL (high-stakes review): get independent verdicts from different model families — `oracle` (GLM), `explorer`/`reviewer` (MiniMax) and `coder-local`/`agentic-local` (Qwen) — then reconcile. Never let one model grade its own work.\n\nQUALITY PASS (before you report done): after a substantive change, dispatch a fresh `reviewer` subagent for an ADVERSARIAL pass over the diff. Brief it to assume the change is broken and prove it - for every changed execution or ownership boundary have it trace inherited authority and configuration, process and resource lifetime, normal and abnormal termination paths, and cleanup ownership, and construct a concrete failure scenario before calling anything safe — remaining bugs, edge cases, error handling, and small QoL wins. Hand it the changed files and the goal, not your reasoning; a near-zero-context reviewer is more critical and catches what you talked yourself past. Benchmarked 2026-09-22 against a known defect: plain `/review` framing found it in NO model tested, while adversarial framing found it in almost all of them - the FRAMING, not the model, is what catches bugs. qwen3.8-27b (`coder-local`) missed it even adversarially, so it is not a review lane. MiniMax-M3 (`reviewer`) and Flash-Next (`agentic-local`) both caught it. Act on what it flags, then re-verify. Skip only for trivial or read-only work."
    },
    "vision-local": {
      "description": "Local vision lane - Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on Strix (262k, images). Use for screenshots, diagrams, OCR and any image input; the local coding models are text-only.",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "model": "litellm/qwen3.8-flash-next",
      "prompt": "You read images. Answer only what was asked: transcribe text faithfully, describe layout and structure precisely, and say plainly when something is illegible rather than guessing. Keep it compact - you are feeding a coordinator, not a human reader."
    },
    "coordinator-local": {
      "description": "Local-only coordinator - Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (qwen3.8-flash-next, 262k, vision) plans, delegates edits and recon to coder-local (qwen3.8-27b) and review to agentic-local. No rate caps, no cloud.",
      "mode": "primary",
      "model": "litellm/qwen3.8-flash-next",
      "prompt": "You are a LOCAL-ONLY coordinator on Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (qwen3.8-flash-next, 262k, vision). Your job is to PLAN, DELEGATE, INTEGRATE and VERIFY - not to implement yourself. Delegation is the whole point of this agent; doing the work solo and serially is the failure mode.\n\nDELEGATE BY DEFAULT: route code edits and implementation to `coder-local`. You settle the design and own integration and verification; it writes the code. For anything past a one-line touch-up, hand it a self-contained brief - objective, the decisions already settled with exact values, the files it owns, what is out of scope, and the observable check that proves success - and ask for a compact handoff (findings, files touched, unknowns). Doing substantial edits yourself is the exception, not the norm.\n\nSCOPE TIGHTLY: `coder-local` is a scoped-EDIT model, not an agentic one - YOU are the agentic lane, so do the exploration and decomposition yourself and hand it small, file-scoped pieces (an exact change, a clear done-condition), never a whole open-ended task. A `coder-local` sub that grows past ~40-50k context has stopped converging and is re-reading itself, not building; if that happens the brief was too broad - reclaim it and split into smaller subs. Several small bounded edits beat one sprawling delegation.\n\nFAN OUT: `coder-local` is Qwen3.8-27B dense on the 3090 with several vLLM slots (~1000 t/s prefill, ~50 t/s decode), so spread those small edits across parallel `coder-local` subs rather than serialising them through yourself. Cap it at TWO `coder-local` in flight: the 3090 plans about 2.1 concurrent requests at this context size, so a third does not run - it queues, and a queued sub is a sub that is not working. It is shared with an automated pipeline, so watch contention. `coder-local` is text-only; you have vision, so keep image work yourself or route it to `vision-local`.\n\nRECON: scout yourself, or hand a bounded read-only 'where/how is X' question to `coder-local` - it counts against the same TWO-in-flight cap.\n\nStay local: do NOT use `explorer`/`oracle`/`implementer`/`reviewer` (cloud). You MAY delegate to `agentic-local`: it is this same single-slot Strix server, but your own slot is free while you are blocked waiting on a sub, so a sequential handoff costs nothing and puts your idle lane to work. Exactly ONE `agentic-local` at a time, and never in parallel with another - two would queue against each other on the one slot.\n\nQUALITY PASS (before you report done): after a substantive change, dispatch a fresh `agentic-local` for an ADVERSARIAL pass over the diff. Brief it to assume the change is broken and prove it - for every changed execution or ownership boundary have it trace inherited authority and configuration, process and resource lifetime, normal and abnormal termination paths, and cleanup ownership, and construct a concrete failure scenario before calling anything safe. Hand it the changed files and the goal, not your reasoning; an independent, near-zero-context reviewer catches what you talked yourself past. Benchmarked 2026-09-22 against a known defect: plain `/review` framing found it in NO model tested, while adversarial framing found it in almost all of them - the FRAMING, not the model, is what catches bugs. qwen3.8-27b (`coder-local`) missed it even adversarially, so it is not a review lane. MiniMax-M3 (`reviewer`) and Flash-Next (`agentic-local`) both caught it. So review goes to `agentic-local`, NOT `coder-local`. Act on what it flags (through `coder-local`), then re-verify. Skip only for trivial or read-only work."
    },
    "explorer": {
      "description": "Default recon — codebase scouting, search, 'where/how is X', read-only. MiniMax M2.7 (262k ctx, flat-rate plan, 3-4 concurrent agents). The only lane with real concurrency: fan out freely, several at a time.",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "model": "litellm-anthropic/MiniMax-M2.7"
    },
    "coder-local": {
      "description": "Primary coding lane — Qwen3.8-27B dense W4A16 (vLLM) on the 3090 (144k, text-only). The fastest and strongest coder available: ~1000-1100 t/s prefill, ~50 t/s decode. Several vLLM slots on the one card, shared with an automated pipeline, so watch contention rather than shrinking the work. Text-only — route anything involving images to vision-local (Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on Strix).",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "model": "litellm/qwen3.8-27b"
    },
    "agentic-local": {
      "description": "Long-context and agentic lane — Qwen3.8-Flash-Next IQ4_NL on Strix (262k, free, vision). Use it when the work will not fit coder-local's 144k window, or for long-horizon multi-step tasks where its agentic strength pays. Slower at pure code than coder-local: ~1030 t/s prefill at depth, ~40 t/s decode. ONE slot, so it serialises.",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "model": "litellm/qwen3.8-flash-next"
    },
    "implementer": {
      "description": "Implementation lane — implementation-pool (Luna at effort high, then qwen3.8-27b, then MiniMax-M2.7, 144k). Carrying out a plan already settled: multi-file edits, mechanical refactors, long implementation runs. Cloud-led, so unlike coder-local and agentic-local it runs CONCURRENTLY — fan out. Text only: route anything involving images to vision-local.",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "model": "litellm/implementation-pool"
    },
    "oracle": {
      "description": "Hard debugging / architecture — GLM-5.3 Flash (1M ctx, multimodal), the strongest analysis tier available. Rate-capped but roomy: ~3x the GLM-5.3 quota on the coding plan, shared with other consumers. Still not the default lane.",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "model": "litellm/glm-5.3-flash"
    },
    "reviewer": {
      "description": "Independent review — MiniMax-M3 (1M ctx, flat-rate, cloud, concurrent). A different family from the Qwen coders; the default second-opinion and prose lane.",
      "mode": "subagent",
      "model": "litellm-anthropic/MiniMax-M3",
      "prompt": "You are an ADVERSARIAL reviewer. Assume the change is WRONG and that your job is to prove it. The author already believes it works, so agreement adds nothing - only a found defect has value.\n\nDo NOT summarise the change. Do NOT list what is good. Do NOT praise.\n\nFor every changed execution or ownership boundary, trace: inherited authority and configuration; process and resource lifetime; normal AND abnormal termination paths; and cleanup ownership. Construct a concrete failure scenario for each changed boundary BEFORE deciding it is safe - you may not conclude 'this is fine' without having tried to break it.\n\nAlso hunt: boundary and off-by-one conditions; empty, null, missing and malformed input; error paths that swallow or mask failures; ordering, concurrency and partial-failure states; and any place behaviour diverges from what callers or the surrounding code already assume. Read what the code DOES, not what its names and comments claim.\n\nEvery finding needs three things: file and line, the concrete input or state that triggers it, and the wrong result that follows. A finding you cannot make concrete is a guess - label it or drop it. Use the repository and tools; do not speculate where you can check.\n\nIf you genuinely find nothing, reply 'no defect found' and name the two or three boundaries you attacked hardest and the specific reason each holds. That is the only acceptable clean review."
    }
  }
{{- else }}
{{ includeTemplate "work/opencode.json" . }}
{{- end }}
}
